        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "For the development of optical lattice clocks."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B008",
    "B001"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Optical lattice clocks are a mature, reproducible advance in precision measurement. Katori and Ye established complementary approaches that made exceptionally accurate optical timekeeping possible. Their demonstrated performance already enables demanding tests of fundamental physics and sensing; the case does not depend on future applications.",
    "case_against": "This recognizes a measurement method rather than a new physical phenomenon, and later performance gains involved a broad community. The citation should therefore remain focused on the foundational clock development, with historical review confirming the distinct contributions of Katori and Ye.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson questioned whether the clocks' reach in fundamental discovery is sufficiently developed compared with a transformative measurement technology.",
        "response": "I understand the distinction, but a measurement advance can itself merit recognition when it is experimentally mature and opens precision regimes that were previously inaccessible. The clocks already support stringent tests of physics, so the case does not rest on discoveries that may come later."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce emphasized that Katori and Ye established complementary methods while cautioning that later increases in precision came from a wider community.",
        "response": "I agree. I would retain both names for the foundational development and keep the citation at that level, without assigning them sole credit for subsequent performance gains. This attribution is appropriately bounded pending historical review of their respective contributions."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "How to distinguish Katori's and Ye's foundational clock advances from later community-wide improvements.",
      "How much weight to give a mature measurement method whose most consequential fundamental discoveries may still lie ahead."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No direct personal or financial connection to Katori or Ye is apparent from the supplied profile. The profile notes institutional overlap through WACQT with Johansson's Chalmers affiliation, but does not identify a connection to B044."
  }
